# Immutable Entities

The following entities are language-independent and form the architectural backbone.

## Source

Any preserved input artifact from which Ma'na derives knowledge.

Examples: manuscript, printed edition, lecture audio, transcription, translation, commentary book, scholarly article, oral recitation, user-submitted note.

## Work

An identifiable cultural or intellectual artifact treated as a coherent object of study.

Examples: ghazal, play, novel, sermon, philosophical dialogue, sacred text, speech.

Important: Work is not limited to poem. Poem is only one subtype.

## Witness

A specific textual or transmitted instance of a work.

Examples: edition, manuscript witness, recited version, translated version, edited publication.

## Segment

Any addressable span inside a witness or source.

Examples: couplet, line, stanza, paragraph, scene, chapter, ayah, hadith passage, section, utterance.

The platform should never assume every work contains couplets. Each parser maps its literary form into Segments.

## Unit

A segment that has been editorially recognized as a meaning-bearing interpretive boundary.

All units are segments. Not all segments are units.

Unit is the main attachment point for commentary, interpretation, and ontology mapping.

## Claim

The smallest active knowledge object in Ma'na.

A claim asserts something about a source, work, witness, unit, ontology entity, relation, or interpretation.

Claim types include: factual, descriptive, interpretive, comparative, historical, ontological, relational, editorial, inferential.

Claims are how Ma'na stores meaning without collapsing facts, interpretations, and AI inferences into one layer.

## Evidence

Any support, constraint, or challenge for a claim.

Examples: quoted textual span, lexical evidence, manuscript evidence, historical documentation, prior commentary, comparative passage, interpretive tradition.

Evidence is what keeps claims reviewable rather than rhetorical.

## Provenance Record

The record of where a claim came from.

Includes: contributor, source, method, timestamp, process, model or tool if any, review history.

Provenance is the governance layer of knowledge.

## Contributor

Any agent capable of producing or editing knowledge objects.

Examples: editor, scholar, curator, translator, reader, AI system, ingestion pipeline.

## Tradition

An interpretive, scholarly, linguistic, religious, or intellectual framework within which claims acquire meaning.

Examples: Sufi commentary, modern literary criticism, philological scholarship, classical tafsir, existentialist reading.

## Ontology Entity

A reusable concept in Ma'na's canonical knowledge space.

Examples: theme, symbol, concept, human experience, literary device, civilization, language, author, collection.

Ontology entities are not generated anew for each work. They are stable references against which works and claims can be mapped.

## Relationship

A meaning-bearing connection between two addressable entities.

Examples: a claim supports another claim, a unit evokes an ontology entity, a work belongs to a collection.

## Question

A preserved inquiry that organizes discovery, interpretation, or retrieval.

Examples: What is exile in Ghalib? Why does hope intensify suffering?

## Commentary Document

A composed, human-readable knowledge package built from claims, evidence, provenance, and references.

Commentary is trustworthy when it is grounded in the text, clear about evidence versus interpretation, historically and culturally aware, citation-friendly, and consistent with editorial standards.

